{"reference":"Matthew 2","book":"Matthew","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"archaeology_geography","title":"Places and movement","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Bethlehem is identified as being in Judea; the phrase locates the town rather than suggesting that it belongs to someone. The chapter then traces a purposeful sequence of places: Bethlehem, Egypt, the land of Israel, Galilee, and Nazareth. On the return, Joseph avoids Judea because Archelaus is reigning there in Herod’s place, and the family withdraws to Galilee, where they settle in Nazareth. These linked place names clarify the narrative’s final destination without turning the account into a travelogue.","verse_refs":["Matthew 2:1","Matthew 2:8-11","Matthew 2:13-15","Matthew 2:19-23","Matthew 2:22","Matthew 2:22-23"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_002_eb8583d60a17df593188d1df","reader_path_matthew_002_80a7ad58c3cff2c4e49c1482","reader_path_matthew_002_b790cebb88c77301d9f34d86","reader_path_matthew_002_4a82a209a293dd9a064a3578","reader_path_matthew_002_5844c32dd64333aadf9bb8cf"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading Matthew’s scriptural quotations","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Matthew presents the escape to Egypt as fulfilling “Out of Egypt did I call my son.” One interpretive approach understands this as a figural rereading: Hosea’s words look back on Israel’s exodus, where Israel is called God’s child, while Matthew sees Jesus’ movement from Egypt as echoing that earlier pattern. This reading does not require treating Hosea’s original reference to Israel as erased.","verse_refs":["Matthew 2:13-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_002_137df95b18ffee81fb0aa1cb"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"The quotation of Jeremiah after the killing at Bethlehem should not automatically be read as a simple one-to-one prediction. Jeremiah has been reread and received in several ways—within biblical interpretation, Jewish textual and liturgical use, New Testament quotation and allusion, and later Christian theology. Keeping these modes distinct helps readers recognize that Matthew’s use can be meaningful without collapsing every later use into direct prediction.","verse_refs":["Matthew 2:17-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_002_d91eb973ca17c12af7eadcd6"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"The Bethlehem citation describes a coming ruler who will shepherd Israel. Matthew’s use belongs to a broader New Testament pattern of reusing or echoing Micah in settings involving Bethlehem, shepherding, household conflict, and Davidic expectation. That wider pattern is an interpretive observation, not a reason to add details beyond what Matthew itself says here.","verse_refs":["Matthew 2:5-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_002_8901779fb26977d5f138e573"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"Details Matthew does and does not give","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Matthew calls the visitors wise-men from the east; it does not number them or call them kings. It narrates their star, gifts, worship, Herod’s alarm, and their return by another route. Matthew alone narrates Herod’s killing of Bethlehem’s young children; although broader Herodian brutality is independently contextualized, this particular event is not independently attested. The chapter also repeatedly frames events with scriptural citations and fulfillment formulas.","verse_refs":["Matthew 2:1","Matthew 2:1-12","Matthew 2:15","Matthew 2:16-18","Matthew 2:17-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_002_0f40692433590dbd43f718d5"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}